00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:41,900
Maybe sometimes even feel pretty pressured into chemotherapy and they're trying to decide

00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:42,900
what to do.

00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:46,460
So let's walk through some steps, I have some rules here.

00:00:46,460 --> 00:00:51,100
So rule number one is to slow down a little bit.

00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:56,940
I know many times it is imperative that you make a decision quickly on the treatment plan

00:00:56,940 --> 00:00:58,500
that you're going to do with your cancer.

00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:03,140
If you have a very aggressive cancer, like a pancreatic cancer, you don't have a lot of

00:01:03,140 --> 00:01:09,780
time to decide on whether you're going to do chemotherapy or not, but still you always

00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:13,740
have more time than they recommend.

00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:18,700
So rule number one is just please, if you're thinking about doing chemotherapy, radiation

00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:24,140
or surgery, usually you have a lot more time that they say you do, slow down a little

00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:27,420
bit and think about it.

00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:32,860
I give the example of one of my patients who was told that if she doesn't do radiation

00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:40,180
immediately, that she has just a couple days to live while she was in the hospital with

00:01:40,180 --> 00:01:44,740
vertebral compression fracture because of cancer had spread to her bones.

00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:50,220
And she was adamant against doing the radiation of the radiologist, oncologist, caveman, and

00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:54,380
said, if you don't do it, you have a couple days to live.

00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:59,380
You call me on the phone and I kind of talked her into maybe it would be in her best interest

00:01:59,380 --> 00:02:03,340
to do the radiation because that's what I believe for this case.

00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:07,020
She had a lot of pain in her back because of the compression fracture.

00:02:07,020 --> 00:02:11,660
And we've just seen good results radiating the spine in a specific spot.

00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:16,500
So she agreed to the radiation after they told her if she didn't do the radiation, she had

00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:18,700
just a couple days to live.

00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:22,100
And then the radiation oncologist said, great, I'll get my nurse in here.

00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:25,940
The nurse came in and said, okay, we could get you started in three weeks.

00:02:25,940 --> 00:02:28,660
She was absolutely living.

00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:33,420
You told me I had three days to live, if I don't do the radiation, I agree to it.

00:02:33,420 --> 00:02:36,500
And you can't get me in to get scheduled to do it for three weeks.

00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:37,500
How does that work?

00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:39,140
Who's telling this truth here?

00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:44,060
Well, she did end up passing away, but she lived six and a half years.

00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:50,020
This is a long time ago after that, you know, metastas to the spine and she had metastas

00:02:50,020 --> 00:02:52,660
several places.

00:02:52,660 --> 00:02:58,660
